--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Game cover art
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The play-page library grid may display game cover images fetched from Valve's
public Steam store endpoints (`store.steampowered.com/api`,
`*.steamstatic.com`) — the same unauthenticated endpoints the Steam website
uses. Images are © their respective rights holders. This lookup is on by
default and can be disabled with `[library] online_art = false`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

InPhase dynamically links and (in packaged builds) bundles third-party software
under its own license terms, notably:

  * GStreamer 1.28.x and related libraries .......... LGPL-2.1-or-later
  * OpenSSL 3 ...................................... Apache-2.0
  * libopus ....................................... BSD-3-Clause
  * libnice ....................................... LGPL-2.1 / MPL-1.1
  * libsrtp and other media libraries ............. BSD-family
  * ~390 Rust crates .............................. mostly MIT / Apache-2.0

No GPL-incompatible component is used or shipped.

Packaged builds ship a complete, per-file manifest with SPDX identifiers and
upstream sources (`MANIFEST.csv`, `OPEN-SOURCE-COMPONENTS.txt`, `licenses/`),
generated from the final runtime bundle.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Codec patents
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

H.264/AVC and H.265/HEVC are covered by patent pools. Building this software for
your own use is one thing; DISTRIBUTING binaries that encode or decode those
formats may carry patent-licensing obligations that this software license does
not and cannot grant — and HEVC's licensing terms are more onerous than
H.264's. Seek your own advice before publishing release binaries. The host
**prefers HEVC** where the browser advertises an HEVC receive codec and falls
back to H.264 automatically; `media.allow_hevc = false` forces H.264 only.
